{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790428313,"nonce":"4Z2Q/5roBIbNxLTm2pUYWrQzsFY=","message":"Stage 3 requirement: the target reaches LLVM, so code is tuned for it.\n\nhost_target_machine takes the default triple with the host CPU and features, and stamp_function stamps those on every function. The AOT mode instead takes a target (triple, CPU, features) from the CLI (`--target`, with `--cpu` / `--target-feature` overrides) and threads it through:\n- TargetMachine from that triple/CPU/features. Native AOT for the host triple defaults to the host CPU. A cross triple defaults to that target's generic CPU unless overridden.\n- The module's triple and data layout come from that machine, set before any IR is built, so the pass pipeline (TTI cost models, vectorizer, InstCombine) optimises for the target and not the host.\n- stamp_function stamps the target's target-cpu / target-features, never the host's. frame-pointer=all stays JIT-only, since wasm has no frame pointer.\n- IR is built for the target's pointer width. On wasm32, pointers are ptr (32-bit) and usize is i32, from Layout::for_triple. Object pointers unboxed from a Value truncate once, not per use.\n- wasm32 feature set: default to what wasmtime and current browsers run (sign-ext, mutable-globals, bulk-memory, nontrapping-fptoint, multivalue, reference-types; simd128 opt-in with a scalar fallback, as the wasm smoke test does). The runtime object is built with the same features (RUSTFLAGS -C target-feature), with the feature list recorded next to it. The linker (stage 4) refuses to join objects whose target_features sections disagree, as wasm-ld does.\n- Tests: the emitted object's triple, and the target-cpu / target-features attributes, match the requested target. A simd128 build emits v128 ops where the host-CPU build would have vectorised.","files":null}]}